{0 Tags, middleware, and converting to typed OCaml}

The previous chapter left [#tag ...] and [^meta ...] values unhandled by
design. This chapter covers resolving them via
{!Eon_edn.Edn_middleware}, then walks through converting a parsed
{!Eon_edn.Edn_effects.value} into a typed OCaml record — using the
companion example
({{:https://github.com/imunitic/eon/blob/main/eon_edn/examples/book/middleware_example.ml}[middleware_example.ml]}).

{1 Tags and middleware}

A tagged value ([#eon/vec2 [1 2]]) performs {!Eon_edn.Edn_effects.Tag}
with [(tag_name, value)]. Drive the parser with
{!Eon_edn.Edn_middleware.run_with_middleware} instead of plain [run] to
resolve it:

{[
  let v =
    Eon_edn.Edn_middleware.run_with_middleware
      (fun () -> Eon_edn.Edn_parser.value ())
      {|#uuid "abc"|}
  in
  match v with
  | Eon_edn.Edn_effects.VTagged ("uuid", VString "abc") -> ()
  | _ -> assert false
  (* no handler registered for "uuid", so it falls back to wrapping the
     tag name and value as-is *)
]}

Register a handler to transform the tagged value instead of leaving it
wrapped:

{[
  let () =
    Eon_edn.Edn_middleware.register_tag_handler "uuid" (fun _ v ->
        match v with
        | Eon_edn.Edn_effects.VString s ->
            Eon_edn.Edn_effects.VTagged ("uuid", VString (String.uppercase_ascii s))
        | v -> v)

  let v =
    Eon_edn.Edn_middleware.run_with_middleware
      (fun () -> Eon_edn.Edn_parser.value ())
      {|#uuid "abc"|}
  in
  match v with
  | Eon_edn.Edn_effects.VTagged ("uuid", VString "ABC") -> ()
  | _ -> assert false
]}

{!Eon_edn.Edn_middleware.register_tag_handler} writes into a process-global
registry — once registered, every later parse in the same process sees the
transformed tag, not just the call site that registered it.

{1 Composing middleware}

{!Eon_edn.Edn_middleware.run_with_middleware}'s [?handlers] takes a
{!Eon_edn.Edn_middleware.handler} list. Each handler can fully resolve a
tag or forward it ([None]) to the next one — either another handler in the
list, or {!Eon_edn.Edn_middleware.default_handler} (the registry lookup
above), which always sits outermost. Two ready-made handlers:

- {!Eon_edn.Edn_middleware.log_tags} — logs every tag, then forwards.
- {!Eon_edn.Edn_middleware.strict_tags} — rejects any tag not in an
  allow-list; allowed tags forward.

{[
  let v =
    Eon_edn.Edn_middleware.run_with_middleware
      ~handlers:Eon_edn.Edn_middleware.[ log_tags; strict_tags [ "uuid" ] ]
      (fun () -> Eon_edn.Edn_parser.value ())
      {|#uuid "abc"|}
  in
  ignore v
]}

Refusal order runs from the {b last} element of [handlers] (nearest the
reader, first crack at each effect) to the {b first} (nearest
{!Eon_edn.Edn_middleware.default_handler}, last crack among the
middleware) — in the example above, [strict_tags] is asked before
[log_tags]. Writing a custom handler follows the same shape: match on
{!Eon_edn.Edn_effects.Tag}, return [Some] to terminate the effect or
[None] to forward it.

{1 Worked example: loading a config file into a typed record}

Everything above stops at {!Eon_edn.Edn_effects.value} — a generic tree.
Getting from there to a real OCaml type (a config record, a component, a
save file) isn't part of [eon-edn] itself; it's a few lines of ordinary
pattern matching that you write once per type. If you're used to
schema-driven or reflection-based (de)serialization from other languages,
this is the part with no equivalent — there's no annotation, no code
generation, no reflection. You write a small function, it's plain code,
you can step through it in a debugger like anything else.

Say you want this EDN file:

{[
  {:name "My Game"
   :width 1920
   :height 1080
   :fullscreen false
   :volume 0.8}
]}

to become this type:

{[
  type config = {
    name : string;
    width : int;
    height : int;
    fullscreen : bool;
    volume : float;
  }
]}

First, small helpers — one to look up a keyword in a map, one per scalar
shape you expect. Both fail loudly (raise) rather than silently
defaulting, so a config file with a missing or wrong-typed field is caught
at load time, not three functions later as a mysterious [0]:

{[
  open Eon_edn.Edn_effects

  let field kvs name =
    match List.assoc_opt (VKeyword name) kvs with
    | Some v -> v
    | None -> failwith (Printf.sprintf "config: missing field %S" name)

  let as_string = function VString s -> s | _ -> failwith "config: expected a string"
  let as_int = function VNumber n -> int_of_float n | _ -> failwith "config: expected a number"
  let as_bool = function VBool b -> b | _ -> failwith "config: expected a boolean"
  let as_float = function VNumber n -> n | _ -> failwith "config: expected a number"
]}

Then the conversion itself — this is the part that's genuinely "yours":

{[
  let config_of_value = function
    | VMap kvs ->
        { name = as_string (field kvs "name")
        ; width = as_int (field kvs "width")
        ; height = as_int (field kvs "height")
        ; fullscreen = as_bool (field kvs "fullscreen")
        ; volume = as_float (field kvs "volume")
        }
    | _ -> failwith "config: expected a top-level map"
]}

And finally, reading it from an actual file — {!Eon_edn.Edn_parser.run}
only needs the file's contents as a string, so this is just an ordinary
[In_channel] read followed by the conversion above:

{[
  let load_config path =
    let contents = In_channel.with_open_bin path In_channel.input_all in
    Eon_edn.Edn_parser.run Eon_edn.Edn_parser.value contents
    |> config_of_value
]}

{[
  let cfg = load_config "game.edn" in
  Printf.printf "%s: %dx%d\n" cfg.name cfg.width cfg.height
]}

That's the whole pattern, and it's the same shape regardless of how
complex the type gets: one small [as_*]/[field] toolkit, one [_of_value]
function per type, composed by hand for nested types (a config with a
sub-record just calls another [_of_value] function on the sub-map). No
macro, no deriving, no build-time codegen step — the tradeoff for that is
exactly this: you write the glue, but the glue is short, obvious, and
entirely under your control.

{1 A more realistic example: nested maps, vectors of maps, and mixed types}

The config above is the easy case — one flat map, one scalar per field.
Most real EDN documents aren't that shape: a save file or a level
description mixes scalars, [VVector]s of strings, and [VVector]s of
{i nested} maps, several levels deep. None of that requires new machinery
— the same [as_*]/[field] toolkit and one [_of_value] function per type
compose exactly the way they did above, just with more of them wired
together.

Say you want to load a small level file:

{[
  {:level "Forest Ruins"
   :entities [{:name "Player"
               :position [3.5 -1.2]
               :tags ["hero" "controllable"]
               :inventory [{:item "Sword" :damage 12 :equipped true}
                           {:item "Potion" :damage 0 :equipped false} ] }
              {:name "Goblin"
               :position [10.0 4.0]
               :tags []
               :inventory [] } ] }
]}

Three nesting levels: a top-level map with an [:entities] field holding a
vector of entity maps, each entity holding both a position vector of two
numbers {i and} an inventory vector of item maps. The target types mirror
that shape directly:

{[
  type item = { item_name : string; damage : int; equipped : bool }

  type entity = {
    entity_name : string;
    position : float * float;
    tags : string list;
    inventory : item list;
  }

  type level = { level_name : string; entities : entity list }
]}

One new helper beyond [field]/[as_string]/[as_int]/[as_bool] from before:
unwrap a [VVector], then map a per-element converter over its contents.
[as_string_list] is just that helper specialised to strings:

{[
  let as_vector = function
    | VVector l -> l
    | _ -> failwith "level: expected a vector"

  let as_string_list v = List.map as_string (as_vector v)
]}

Each [_of_value] function only ever looks one level deep — [entity_of_value]
doesn't know or care how [item_of_value] works, it just calls it once per
element of the inventory vector. A 2-element position vector gets its own
tiny converter rather than being folded into a generic "vector of numbers"
helper, since a position specifically wants a pair, not a list:

{[
  let item_of_value = function
    | VMap kvs ->
        { item_name = as_string (field kvs "item")
        ; damage = as_int (field kvs "damage")
        ; equipped = as_bool (field kvs "equipped")
        }
    | _ -> failwith "item: expected a map"

  let position_of_value = function
    | VVector [ VNumber x; VNumber y ] -> (x, y)
    | _ -> failwith "entity: expected a 2-element position vector"

  let entity_of_value = function
    | VMap kvs ->
        { entity_name = as_string (field kvs "name")
        ; position = position_of_value (field kvs "position")
        ; tags = as_string_list (field kvs "tags")
        ; inventory = List.map item_of_value (as_vector (field kvs "inventory"))
        }
    | _ -> failwith "entity: expected a map"

  let level_of_value = function
    | VMap kvs ->
        { level_name = as_string (field kvs "level")
        ; entities = List.map entity_of_value (as_vector (field kvs "entities"))
        }
    | _ -> failwith "level: expected a top-level map"
]}

[level_of_value] reads top-down but {i executes} bottom-up: parsing the
example file above calls [item_of_value] twice (once per inventory entry),
[entity_of_value] twice (once per entity), and [level_of_value] once — each
failure still fails loudly and locally (a malformed item raises from
[item_of_value] itself, not from some generic catch-all three frames up).
This is the whole answer to "what about nested/mixed real-world data": no
new API, just more instances of the same [_of_value] pattern, called from
each other in the shape of the data.

